The angle weight (≈7) is kept high because all successful runs required a strong positive gain on the pole angle to generate sufficient torque and keep the pole upright. The cart‑position weight is set to –2.0 (more negative than previously tried) to apply a leftward force when the cart drifts right, directly counteracting the persistent rightward bias observed in earlier trials. The cart‑velocity weight remains –0.5 to provide damping against rapid movements, while the angular‑velocity weight stays at 2.0 to assist the controller in reacting to the pole’s swing. Finally, a bias of –0.5 introduces a constant small leftward push that helps offset any residual rightward drift without overwhelming the feedback gains. This combination balances strong angle correction with position feedback and modest damping, increasing the chance of sustaining the pole for many more steps and moving toward the target reward of ~1000.
