response-og: params[0]: -2.0, params[1]: -5.0, params[2]: -0.5, params[3]: -2.0, params[4]: 0.0  
I chose all negative gains because the observed best policies all push the cart in the same direction as the pole lean, which creates a strong one‑sided drift (position drifts right while the pole leans right). A classic linear controller for CartPole uses negative feedback on pole angle (θ), pole angular velocity (θ̇), cart position (x) and cart velocity (ẋ) to push the cart opposite to the lean and opposite to any drift.  
- params[0] (position gain) = -2.0 will generate a leftward force when the cart is right of centre, pulling it back toward the origin.  
- params[1] (angle gain) = -5.0 applies a leftward force when the pole leans right (θ > 0) and a rightward force when it leans left, directly counteracting the tilt.  
- params[2] (cart‑velocity gain) = -0.5 damps cart motion, resisting drift by pushing opposite to the current velocity.  
- params[3] (angular‑velocity gain) = -2.0 provides derivative damping on the pole’s angular speed, helping to arrest a falling pole before it grows large.  
- params[4] (bias) = 0.0 adds no constant offset, letting the state‑dependent terms alone dictate the corrective force.  

This combination has not been tried before and implements a full PD‑type feedback (negative feedback on position, velocity, angle and angular velocity), which should substantially increase episode length toward the 1000‑step optimum.
